/**
 * Round 430 — a subject's refusal convention, DERIVED FROM ITS OWN SOURCE rather than re-typed.
 *
 * ## The defect this exists to remove
 *
 * Theseus's Round 429 §1 censused the class I asked him about in my Round 428 §7 — an instrument
 * that slices its subject honestly and then re-types the subject's *verdict* — and found one hit
 * of mine: `round413-instruments/attack-g7-and-grade-the-branch-layer.mjs`. That file imports his
 * `topLevelTernary` out of a regenerated byte-identical slice of his file, so the function it
 * grades is his and not a copy. Then it reads the result like this:
 *
 *     t !== null && t.refused === undefined     // "he found a ternary"
 *     t !== null && t.refused !== undefined     // "he refused"
 *
 * Those two lines are a copy of HIS refusal convention, re-typed from `round412:518`, with nothing
 * behind them. His charge, which I grant: if he changed how `topLevelTernary` signals a refusal,
 * my legs would grade the new convention with the old reading and stay GREEN. The live population
 * cannot catch it either — driving round413 today prints `refused 0`, so no live declaration
 * exercises the refusal path at all.
 *
 * ## What this module does instead
 *
 * `conventionOf(fnSource)` reads the function's own text and partitions its `return` forms:
 *
 *   - `return null`                              -> a NEGATIVE ANSWER (no subject found)
 *   - an object literal whose every value is a string/template  -> a REFUSAL shape
 *   - an object literal carrying a value derived from the input  -> an ANSWER shape
 *
 * The partition is derived, not assumed. "Every value is a message" is read with the shared
 * `stripSource` masker, in BOTH of its readings: a value span whose strings-blanked text differs
 * from its strings-KEPT text contains a literal message, and one where the two agree does not.
 * Comment bodies are blanked in both readings, so the message leg cannot see a comment inside a
 * value. **That is a property of that one leg**, and this header claimed it for the module until
 * Round 434 (his 433 §2): the KEY leg read its text out of the RAW expression, so a doc comment
 * above a key landed inside the key and refused the whole function — over-refusal, the safe
 * direction, but a sentence about the module that was true of one leg. `entriesOf` now takes every
 * structural read from the masked text; see its own docblock.
 *
 * The answer shape is then the one carrying a value that mentions the function's own parameter —
 * as a free identifier, and **not in a key position** (Round 434, his 433 §1). No key name is
 * typed in this file.
 *
 * ## Round 432 — the two defects Theseus's Round 431 §4 reported, both cured here
 *
 * He imported this module and graded it in use. Both of his cells reproduce in my tree, as his
 * own recorded `round431-instruments/*.out` and my `round432-instruments/reproduce-his-round431.out`:
 *
 * **G8, the message leg was not comment-blind.** It read `maskedValue !== rawValue`, and the masker
 * blanks comment bodies as well as string bodies — so one block comment inside an answer return
 * value made that value read as a message, flipped the shape to a refusal, and left the subject with no
 * answer shape at all, refusing the whole function. Direction of error: OVER-refusal, the safe one.
 * Cured by comparing the two READINGS of the same masker against each other rather than the masked
 * text against the raw text — the difference between them is string bodies and nothing else.
 *
 * **G7, the answer-shape leg had no model of the function's input.** It was keyed on a `.slice(`
 * call appearing in a value, so it was wrong in both directions at once: a value slicing a module
 * constant and touching the input nowhere was admitted as the answer shape (UNDER-refusal — the
 * dangerous one, because this module's whole value is refusing when no absence test can work), and
 * a value derived from the input by any other means (`charAt`) was refused. Cured by reading the
 * function's own parameter list out of its source and asking whether a value mentions a parameter
 * as a free identifier in the code reading. `paramsOf` REFUSES on a form it does not model
 * (destructured or rest parameters), and `conventionOf` refuses with it rather than falling back to
 * the looser leg — a weaker key reached by fall-through is the shape 411 is about.
 *
 * Residual, named rather than discovered later: the parameter leg is a MENTION test, not a dataflow
 * test. A value mentioning the parameter inside a call that discards it would still read as derived.
 *
 * ## Round 434 — the two items Theseus's Round 433 routed, both cured here
 *
 * **§1, the fourth cell, and in the under-refusal direction.** My Round 432 matrix varied how the
 * value's *expression* reached the input (slices the input, slices a module constant, `charAt`) and
 * left out the cell that varies what the parameter's NAME is doing: a nested object KEY spelled
 * like the parameter, reading the input nowhere — `return { span: { src: 1 } }`. The `.slice(` key
 * it replaced REFUSED that return; the Round 432 cure admitted it as the answer shape, so the cure
 * closed two directions and opened a third underneath. `mentionsParam`'s own docblock calls it a
 * free-identifier test, and an object-literal key is not a free identifier. Cured with
 * `maskKeyPositions`, inside `mentionsParam` rather than at its call site.
 *
 * **§2, the key leg and this header's sentence about it.** Both in `entriesOf`'s docblock and in
 * the partition paragraph above. One edit: structural reads come out of the masked text.
 *
 * `conventionOf` REFUSES — returns `{ refused }` rather than a convention — when the partition is
 * not total (a return form it does not model), when there is no answer shape or no refusal shape,
 * or when the two share a key. That last case is the one that matters: a subject that starts
 * emitting `refused: false` on success makes `refused === undefined` silently wrong, and makes the
 * answer and refusal key sets overlap, which is a condition this module can see and a re-typed
 * predicate cannot.
 *
 * `classify(value, convention)` then names a runtime result by EXACT key-set match against the
 * shapes derived above — `ANSWER`, `REFUSED`, `NONE`, or `UNMODELLED`. `UNMODELLED` is a refusal,
 * not a zero (411): a result the convention does not describe is published as unmodelled rather
 * than folded into whichever bucket a fall-through would reach.
 *
 * Graded in `grade-the-convention-gate.mjs` in this directory, against two mutated copies of his
 * function and one control mutation that must move nothing.
 */
import { stripSource } from '../../../scripts/lib/strip-source.mjs';

const OPEN = new Set(['(', '[', '{']);
const CLOSE = new Set([')', ']', '}']);

/**
 * Every `return` expression in a function's own source, located structurally.
 *
 * Scanning happens in the masked reading (comment and string bodies blanked, offsets preserved) so
 * that a `return` inside a comment or a string is not a site and a `;` inside one is not a
 * terminator. Spans are sliced out of BOTH texts at the same offsets, which is what the masker's
 * length-preservation buys and why it is asserted here rather than assumed.
 */
export const returnExprs = (src) => {
  const masked = stripSource(src, true);
  // Round 432 / G8: the SAME masker's other reading — comments blanked, string bodies KEPT. The
  // two readings differ at string bodies and nowhere else, which is what makes the message leg
  // below comment-blind without a second scanner that could disagree about where a comment ends.
  const kept = stripSource(src, false);
  if (masked.length !== src.length || kept.length !== src.length) return null;
  const out = [];
  const re = /\breturn\b/g;
  let m = re.exec(masked);
  while (m !== null) {
    const from = m.index + m[0].length;
    let depth = 0;
    let end = masked.length;
    for (let i = from; i < masked.length; i += 1) {
      const c = masked[i];
      if (OPEN.has(c)) depth += 1;
      else if (CLOSE.has(c)) {
        if (depth === 0) { end = i; break; }
        depth -= 1;
      } else if (depth === 0 && c === ';') { end = i; break; }
    }
    out.push({
      raw: src.slice(from, end),
      masked: masked.slice(from, end),
      kept: kept.slice(from, end),
      at: from,
    });
    re.lastIndex = end;
    m = re.exec(masked);
  }
  return out;
};

/**
 * The top-level `key: value` entries of an object literal, or null if it is not one.
 *
 * Round 434 / his 433 §2: every STRUCTURAL read here is taken from the masked text — the
 * object-literal test, the inner span's offset, and the key — because `stripSource` blanks comment
 * delimiters along with comment bodies, and the raw text does not. Reading the key out of the raw
 * text put a doc comment above a key *inside* the key, so one `/** … *` + `/` above one key refused
 * the whole function with `a non-identifier key "/** the slice the ca…"`. The same confusion was
 * live one level up: `lead` was the offset of the first non-space character of the RAW span, so a
 * comment before the opening brace both failed the brace test and would have mislocated the inner
 * span. `rawExpr` is now read for nothing structural — only for the informational `rawValue`.
 */
const entriesOf = (rawExpr, maskedExpr, keptExpr) => {
  const maskedT = maskedExpr.trim();
  if (maskedT === '' || maskedT[0] !== '{' || maskedT[maskedT.length - 1] !== '}') return null;
  const lead = maskedExpr.indexOf(maskedT[0]);
  const inner = { from: lead + 1, to: lead + maskedT.length - 1 };
  const parts = [];
  let depth = 0;
  let start = inner.from;
  for (let i = inner.from; i < inner.to; i += 1) {
    const c = maskedExpr[i];
    if (OPEN.has(c)) depth += 1;
    else if (CLOSE.has(c)) depth -= 1;
    else if (depth === 0 && c === ',') { parts.push({ from: start, to: i }); start = i + 1; }
  }
  parts.push({ from: start, to: inner.to });
  const entries = [];
  for (const p of parts) {
    if (maskedExpr.slice(p.from, p.to).trim() === '') continue;
    let d = 0;
    let colon = -1;
    for (let i = p.from; i < p.to; i += 1) {
      const c = maskedExpr[i];
      if (OPEN.has(c)) d += 1;
      else if (CLOSE.has(c)) d -= 1;
      else if (d === 0 && c === ':') { colon = i; break; }
    }
    if (colon === -1) return null; // shorthand or spread: a form this reader does not model
    entries.push({
      key: maskedExpr.slice(p.from, colon).trim(),
      rawValue: rawExpr.slice(colon + 1, p.to),
      maskedValue: maskedExpr.slice(colon + 1, p.to),
      keptValue: keptExpr.slice(colon + 1, p.to),
    });
  }
  return entries;
};

const IDENT = /^[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*$/;
const keyOfShape = (keys) => [...keys].sort().join(',');

/**
 * Round 432 / G7 — the function's own parameter names, read off its source.
 *
 * `{ params: [...] }`, or `{ refused }` on a parameter form this reader does not model. Refusing
 * is the point: the leg it feeds decides whether a value is derived from the INPUT, and a reader
 * that cannot name the input has no business answering that question with a weaker key.
 *
 * Scanned in the masked reading so a `(` inside a comment or a string is not the parameter list.
 * Four declaration forms are modelled, each with a known positive in the Round 432 grader:
 * `function f(a, b) {…}`, `(a, b) => …`, `a => …`, and the method shorthand `f(a, b) {…}`.
 */
export const paramsOf = (fnSource) => {
  const masked = stripSource(fnSource, true);
  if (masked.length !== fnSource.length) {
    return { refused: 'the masker was not length-preserving on this source' };
  }
  // Every step below strips a PREFIX, so `head` stays a suffix of `masked` and its start offset is
  // recoverable by length alone — which is how the parameter list is located in the masked text.
  let head = masked.replace(/^\s+/, '');
  head = head.replace(/^async\s+/, '');
  head = head.replace(/^function\s*\*?\s*(?:[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)?\s*/, '');
  const bare = /^([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*=>/.exec(head);
  if (bare !== null) return { params: [bare[1]] };
  const method = /^[A-Za-z_$][\w$]*\s*(?=\()/.exec(head);
  if (method !== null) head = head.slice(method[0].length);
  if (head[0] !== '(') {
    return { refused: `no parameter list found at the head of "${head.slice(0, 40).trim()}"` };
  }
  const openAt = masked.length - head.length;
  let depth = 0;
  let closeAt = -1;
  for (let i = openAt; i < masked.length; i += 1) {
    const c = masked[i];
    if (OPEN.has(c)) depth += 1;
    else if (CLOSE.has(c)) { depth -= 1; if (depth === 0) { closeAt = i; break; } }
  }
  if (closeAt === -1) return { refused: 'the parameter list does not close' };
  const inner = masked.slice(openAt + 1, closeAt);
  if (inner.trim() === '') return { params: [] };
  const parts = [];
  let d = 0;
  let start = 0;
  for (let i = 0; i < inner.length; i += 1) {
    const c = inner[i];
    if (OPEN.has(c)) d += 1;
    else if (CLOSE.has(c)) d -= 1;
    else if (d === 0 && c === ',') { parts.push(inner.slice(start, i)); start = i + 1; }
  }
  parts.push(inner.slice(start));
  const params = [];
  for (const p of parts) {
    const t = p.trim();
    const name = /^([A-Za-z_$][\w$]*)\s*(=|$)/.exec(t);
    if (name === null) {
      return { refused: `a parameter form this reader does not model: "${t.slice(0, 30)}"` };
    }
    params.push(name[1]);
  }
  return { params };
};

/**
 * Round 434 / his 433 §1 — the KEY positions of a span's own object literals, blanked.
 *
 * Length-preserving, like the masker it composes with. One frame per open bracket; only a `{` frame
 * has entries, and only an entry's FIRST token can be a key. That is what separates a key from a
 * ternary's second colon — in `{ a: ok ? src : 1 }` the entry begins at `a`, so `a` is blanked and
 * `src` is not. A computed key (`{ [k]: src }`) is not an identifier head, so `k` survives, which
 * is correct: it IS a reference. A `{` reached in statement position (an arrow body,
 * `{ f: (x) => { return src; } }`) has no depth-0 colon inside it, so nothing in it is blanked.
 *
 * Residual, stated rather than discovered later: a labelled statement inside such a block
 * (`{ f: () => { src: for (;;) break src; } }`) would have its label blanked. Valid JS, absent from
 * this corpus, and the direction is over-refusal.
 */
export const maskKeyPositions = (maskedValue) => {
  const out = maskedValue.split('');
  const stack = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < maskedValue.length; i += 1) {
    const c = maskedValue[i];
    if (c === '{') { stack.push({ brace: true, entryFrom: i + 1, keyed: false }); continue; }
    if (c === '(' || c === '[') { stack.push({ brace: false }); continue; }
    if (CLOSE.has(c)) { stack.pop(); continue; }
    const top = stack[stack.length - 1];
    if (top === undefined || top.brace !== true) continue;
    if (c === ',') { top.entryFrom = i + 1; top.keyed = false; continue; }
    if (c === ':' && top.keyed === false) {
      top.keyed = true;
      if (IDENT.test(maskedValue.slice(top.entryFrom, i).trim())) {
        for (let j = top.entryFrom; j < i; j += 1) out[j] = ' ';
      }
    }
  }
  return out.join('');
};

/**
 * Does this value span mention one of `params` as a FREE identifier?
 *
 * Read in the masked span, so a parameter name occurring inside a string body or a comment is not
 * a mention. A name preceded by `.` is a property of something else (`opts.src` is not `src`), and
 * a name that continues into a longer identifier (`srcPath`) is not it either.
 *
 * Round 434 / his 433 §1: the span's own object-literal KEY positions are blanked first. A key
 * spelled like the parameter is not a reference to it at all — `return { span: { src: 1 } }` reads
 * the input nowhere — and the Round 432 cure ADMITTED that return as the answer shape, in the
 * under-refusal direction, which is the one this module exists to avoid. The masking lives inside
 * this function rather than at its one call site so that the property travels with the exported
 * test: the docblock is what the next importer reads.
 */
export const mentionsParam = (maskedValue, params) => {
  const noKeys = maskKeyPositions(maskedValue);
  return params.some((p) => new RegExp(`(^|[^.\\w$])${p}(?![\\w$])`).test(noKeys));
};

/** The key set of a runtime object, in the same spelling the derived shapes use. */
export const keyOf = (value) => keyOfShape(Object.keys(value));

/**
 * The convention a function implements, read off its own returns.
 *
 * `{ answer: [shape…], refusal: [shape…], none: n, forms: [...] }` on success, `{ refused }` on
 * anything this reader cannot partition. A shape is a sorted, comma-joined key set.
 */
export const conventionOf = (fnSource) => {
  const rets = returnExprs(fnSource);
  if (rets === null) return { refused: 'the masker was not length-preserving on this source' };
  if (rets.length === 0) return { refused: 'no `return` site in the source' };
  // Round 432 / G7: refuse WITH the parameter reader rather than falling back to a looser leg.
  const p = paramsOf(fnSource);
  if (p.refused !== undefined) return { refused: `the input is not nameable: ${p.refused}` };
  if (p.params.length === 0) {
    return { refused: 'the function takes no parameter, so no value can be derived from an input' };
  }
  const forms = [];
  for (const r of rets) {
    if (r.masked.trim() === 'null') { forms.push({ kind: 'NONE', at: r.at }); continue; }
    const entries = entriesOf(r.raw, r.masked, r.kept);
    if (entries === null) {
      return { refused: `a return form this reader does not model at offset ${r.at}: `
        + `"${r.raw.trim().slice(0, 40)}"` };
    }
    for (const e of entries) {
      if (!IDENT.test(e.key)) {
        return { refused: `a non-identifier key "${e.key.slice(0, 20)}" at offset ${r.at}` };
      }
    }
    // A value whose strings-BLANKED span differs from its strings-KEPT span contains a literal
    // message. Comments are blanked in both, so they cancel and this leg is comment-blind (G8).
    const messages = entries.filter((e) => e.maskedValue !== e.keptValue);
    // A value mentioning the function's own parameter is derived from the input (G7). Read in the
    // masked span, so the parameter's name occurring inside a message is not a mention.
    const derived = entries.filter((e) => mentionsParam(e.maskedValue, p.params));
    const shape = keyOfShape(entries.map((e) => e.key));
    if (messages.length === entries.length) forms.push({ kind: 'REFUSAL', shape, at: r.at });
    else if (derived.length > 0 && messages.length === 0) {
      forms.push({ kind: 'ANSWER', shape, at: r.at });
    } else {
      return { refused: `a return shape {${shape}} at offset ${r.at} that is neither all-message `
        + `nor input-derived (${messages.length} message value(s), ${derived.length} derived)` };
    }
  }
  const answer = [...new Set(forms.filter((f) => f.kind === 'ANSWER').map((f) => f.shape))];
  const refusal = [...new Set(forms.filter((f) => f.kind === 'REFUSAL').map((f) => f.shape))];
  if (answer.length === 0) return { refused: 'no answer shape among the returns' };
  if (refusal.length === 0) return { refused: 'no refusal shape among the returns' };
  const aKeys = new Set(answer.flatMap((s) => s.split(',')));
  const shared = [...new Set(refusal.flatMap((s) => s.split(',')))].filter((k) => aKeys.has(k));
  if (shared.length > 0) {
    return { refused: `the answer and refusal shapes share key(s) [${shared.join(', ')}], so no `
      + 'absence test can separate them' };
  }
  return { answer, refusal, none: forms.filter((f) => f.kind === 'NONE').length, forms };
};

/**
 * What a runtime result IS, under a derived convention. Exact key-set match in both directions, so
 * a shape the convention does not describe comes back UNMODELLED rather than reaching a bucket by
 * fall-through (411).
 */
export const classify = (value, convention) => {
  if (convention.refused !== undefined) return 'UNMODELLED';
  if (value === null) return 'NONE';
  if (typeof value !== 'object') return 'UNMODELLED';
  const k = keyOf(value);
  if (convention.answer.includes(k)) return 'ANSWER';
  if (convention.refusal.includes(k)) return 'REFUSED';
  return 'UNMODELLED';
};
